From fb94e614565c02c1f267d4c376f661c335041d22 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Alan Rockefeller <alanrockefeller@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 7 Oct 2026 09:33:35 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Exact AVX2 speedups for x86 without AVX-512 (Broadwell),
 7.526-opt5-dikarya1

Same constraint as the opt series: output byte-identical to the stock gcc
build at --thread 1.  All paths are guarded by __AVX2__ && !MAFFT_STOCK_FMA
(and !__AVX512F__ where an AVX-512 path exists), so other builds are
unchanged.

- Lfill_int (L-INS-i all-pairs local alignment): AVX2 fill, 8 cells per
  step.  Cells whose best move is a gap store a marker instead of the gap
  offset, all DP rows are kept, and Ltracking replays the original scalar
  rule over the stored rows for the few marked cells on its path (integer
  arithmetic, so the offsets are exact).  This drops the first-index scan
  and the vmp[] state from the inner loop: 1.68 -> 0.82 ns/cell.
- scarr_fill(): the 26x26 profile-score loop of every match_calc, skipping
  the zero terms (exact: the sum starts at +0 and is never -0) and adding
  4 letters at a time with separate multiply and add.  Also mc_match.
- A_row / partA_row: AVX2 ports of the AVX-512 row fills.
- Falign: per-call result/tmpres/tmpseq/rndseq rows are malloc'd, not
  calloc'd (AllocateCharMtxNoZero); they are only used as strings written
  before they are read.  MAFFT_POISON_TEST fills them with junk to check.

Verified byte-identical to stock MAFFT 7.505 (Ubuntu) / 7.526 at --thread 1
on 52 real Dikarya inputs (L-INS-i and FFT-NS-i), with and without
MAFFT_POISON_TEST.

Hardware: Intel Xeon E5-2690 v4 (Broadwell-EP, AVX2 + FMA, no AVX-512),
gcc 13.3, -O3 -march=native, Ubuntu 24.04.

Builds on the opt1-opt5 series by Josh Walker and Claude Code.

Co-authored-by: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01DB1sfN8zWs4Fi4QfWU5CP8
